From jrp@jrp.tiac.net Mon Jun 16 00:28:31 1997
Message-ID: <3.0.1.32.19970616004523.00a4fce8@pop.bart.nl>
Date: Mon, 16 Jun 1997 00:23:10 -0400
From: Jim Paradis <jrp@jrp.tiac.net>
Subject: Re: Compiling 64 bit code

Thomas Pornin wrote:

Actually, I believe using 32 bits operands is better than 64 bits ones

on an alpha when only the first 32 bits are used. You may use less

opcodes to fill a register, and the memory accesses are faster (as
long

as the 32 bits words are aligned on doublewords boundaries). Assembly

instructions handle well the 32 bits case.

Not entirely true.  32-bit memory accesses take exactly the same
amount

of time as 64-bit memory accesses.  The primary advantage to using
32 bits

is that you can pack twice as many of them into a cache block, thus
reducing

the number of cache misses.

On the possible downside, the LDL instruction automatically sign-extends

the 32-bit operand into the 64-bit register.  This may or may
not be desired

behavior depending on what you are doing (okay for signed arithmetic,
not so

okay for pointers or mixed 32/64-bit unsigned calculations). 
I believe, though,

that a ZAP instruction will multiple-issue along with an LDL, so if
it's important

that you get unsigned behavior you can do it this way with minimal
performance

penalty...

 

-- 
